   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the state finance law, in relation to the rededication of unused funds in the New York state local government records management improvement fund",
    "basePrintNo": "S3307",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3307-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 43,
       "fullName": "Kathleen A. Marchione",
       "imgName": "435_kathleen_a._marchione.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 435,
       "sessionMemberId": 940,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "MARCHIONE"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §97-i, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3307\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the state finance law, in relation\nto the rededication of unused funds in the New York state local\ngovernment records management improvement fund\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo require the rededication of unused funds in the New York state\nlocal government records management improvement fund\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends § 94-i of the State Finance Law by adding a new\nsubdivision stating that in all cases, no monies remaining in the fund\nshall be transferred or allocated, except for the purposes provided\nfor under this section.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nSection 97-i of the State Finance law creates the New York State Local\nGovernment Management Improvement Fund consisting of revenues derived\nfrom the imposition of certain Department of Motor Vehicle fees issued\nby county clerks' offices.\n\nThe fees supporting the New York State Local Government Management\nImprovement Fund are pursuant to §§ 8018(a)(3), 8021(a)(4)(b),\n8021(b)(11)(b) of the Civil Practice Law and Rules, § 7-614(1) of the\nNew York City Administrative Code, and § 57.35 of the Arts and\nCultural Affairs Law. These fees include index number fees, fees for\nservices rendered by a clerk in his or her capacity as clerk of the\nsupreme or county court, and fees for filing federal tax lien\npayments.\n\nSection 57.35 of the Arts and Cultural Affairs Law provides the grant\nprogram for local government records management improvement. This\nsection authorizes the Commissioner of Education, upon consultation\nwith the local government records advisory council to award grants for\nrecords management. The commissioner is charged with promulgating the\nrules and regulations setting forth the criteria and procedures\nnecessary to award such grants. This section also provides certain\ncriteria that must be included in the competitive grant program.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nElectronic records management is a major challenge for local\ngovernments and is generally the weakest part of most local government\nrecords management programs. The New York State Local Government\nRecords Management Improvement Fund grant program is designed to help\nlocal governments address their electronic records management needs.\nThe goal of the grant is to finance projects addressing electronic\nrecords management issues and produce results, best practices, and\nmodels that can be replicated, are scalable, or can be used more\nbroadly in other governments or groups of governments of all types and\nsizes. This legislation ensures that remaining funds in the Records\nManagement Improvement Fund are rededicated to the Fund in support of\nthe fund's objectives.\n\nIn the past, unused funding from the Records Management Improvement\nFund was diverted into the State's general fund to address any short\nfalls in the state's budget. This redirection of funding caused a\nsignificant reduction in future grants programs. Diverting the\ndedicated funds to other State operations is contrary to the\n\ncommitment made by the leaders of our state that these fees would be\ndedicated to the improvement of records management.\n\nThe Fund is collected by counties and used to support grant programs\naimed at improving records management by New York State's local\ngovernments. It is a competitive program, awarding grants based on the\nmerits of applications. The amount of grant funding available each\nyear depends on the number of documents recorded and index numbers\nassigned that year.\n\nThese adverse effects of sweeping the Fund will be prevented by the\nmandatory rededication of unused funds to the New York State Local\nGovernment Records Management Improvement Fund. Ensuring that the\nunused funding is rededicated to the Fund for the purpose of\nsupporting the Fund's grants program is essential to the proliferation\nof the program and resolving the challenging issues related to\nelectronic records management. For the aforementioned reasons it is\nessential that this bill is enacted.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2016: S.6708A\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
